  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) A mid-level trough will shift east through the Ohio \nValley this afternoon into tonight. Ahead of the trough, scattered \nshowers and storms are expected this afternoon, primary over central \nand south- central Ohio, as modest instability develops and height \nfalls occur. This activity will weaken and quickly exit to the east \nafter sunset. Mid- level ridging will move in right behind the trough\non Friday, with high temperatures once again lifting into the 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) A stronger trough will approach our area Saturday.\nCurrent timing has the associated cold front moving through early in\nthe day for west-central Ohio, and departing our central/south-\ncentral OH and northeast Kentucky counties by late afternoon or early\nevening. If this timing holds, the best chance for thunderstorms \nwould be for our eastern and southern areas during the afternoon. \nVertical wind fields are impressive with this system, and we are \nlikely to have strong forcing along the surface front. Severe storms \nare possible, but will be strongly dependent on how much instability \nis able to develop ahead of the front. \n\nCAA will bring much cooler weather Sunday and Monday. Frost continues\nto be possible Sunday night if winds can slacken enough, with a\nfreeze also possible across northern areas.",
